<ns0:section xmlns:ns0="http://xml.house.gov/schemas/uslm/1.0" style="-uslm-lc:I80" id="idb9beb12c-47c4-11f1-9a61-b1559996dfe8" identifier="/us/usc/t18/s3733"><ns0:num value="3733">§ 3733.</ns0:num><ns0:heading> Assignment of errors—(Rule)</ns0:heading><ns0:note id="idb9beb12d-47c4-11f1-9a61-b1559996dfe8">
<ns0:heading style="-uslm-lc:I71" class="centered"><ns0:inline class="small-caps">See Federal Rules of Criminal Procedure</ns0:inline></ns0:heading>
<ns0:p style="-uslm-lc:I21" class="indent0 fontsize7">Assignments of error on appeal abolished, Rule 37(a)(1).</ns0:p>
<ns0:p style="-uslm-lc:I21" class="indent0 fontsize7">Necessity of specific objection in order to assign error in instructions, Rule 30.</ns0:p>
</ns0:note>
<ns0:sourceCredit id="idb9beb12e-47c4-11f1-9a61-b1559996dfe8">(<ns0:ref href="/us/act/1948-06-25/ch645">June 25, 1948, ch. 645</ns0:ref>, <ns0:ref href="/us/stat/62/845">62 Stat. 845</ns0:ref>.)</ns0:sourceCredit>
<ns0:notes type="uscNote" id="idb9beb12f-47c4-11f1-9a61-b1559996dfe8">
<ns0:note style="-uslm-lc:I74" role="crossHeading" topic="editorialNotes" id="idb9beb130-47c4-11f1-9a61-b1559996dfe8"><ns0:heading class="centered"><ns0:b>Editorial Notes</ns0:b></ns0:heading></ns0:note>
<ns0:note style="-uslm-lc:I75" topic="referencesInText" id="idb9beb131-47c4-11f1-9a61-b1559996dfe8">
<ns0:heading class="centered smallCaps">References in Text</ns0:heading><ns0:p style="-uslm-lc:I21" class="indent0">Rule 37 of the Federal Rules of Criminal Procedure was abrogated <ns0:date date="1947-12-04">Dec. 4, 1947</ns0:date>, eff. <ns0:date date="1968-07-01">July 1, 1968</ns0:date>, and is covered by Rule 3, Federal Rules of Appellate Procedure, set out in the Appendix to Title 28, Judiciary and Judicial Procedure.</ns0:p>
</ns0:note>
</ns0:notes>
</ns0:section>
